    "miraclesAndDevotion": "Devotion to St. Catherine de' Ricci has flourished because the faithful experience this saint as a powerful intercessor before God. Patron of the sick, Dominican tertiaries, and Florence, St. Catherine de' Ricci is invoked in novenas, parish feast-day celebrations, and private prayer — especially by those who see their struggles reflected in this saint's life. Dominican mystic who experienced weekly ecstasies of the Passion for twelve years Bilocation miracles were reported during her ecstasies Served as prioress of San Vincenzo convent in Prato for thirty-six years Shrines, relics, and icons associated with this saint attract pilgrims seeking healing, guidance, and conversion. Popular piety sometimes preserves customs linked to this feast: special foods, processions, school celebrations, or charitable collections for the causes this saint championed. The Church distinguishes between public revelation and private devotion, yet countless Catholics testify that praying through St. Catherine de' Ricci opened doors when human effort alone failed. That living tradition — not merely historical curiosity — is why \"Saint of the Day\" features remain among the most searched Catholic topics online.",
    "patronages": "St. Catherine de' Ricci is widely invoked as patron of the sick, Dominican tertiaries, and Florence. Patron saints function in Catholic spirituality as elder siblings in faith: they have triumphed, they understand human weakness, and they present our needs before Christ. Choosing St. Catherine de' Ricci as a heavenly advocate is never superstition; it is the communion of saints made practical. The sick find particular comfort under this patronage, but the Church's calendar teaches that every saint overflows with grace for anyone who asks. On February 13, consider entrusting a specific intention — health, employment, conversion of a loved one, peace in your nation — to St. Catherine de' Ricci. Many Catholics also name children, parishes, or apostolates after their patron saint, creating a lifelong bond of prayer and imitation.",
    "legacy": "The legacy of St. Catherine de' Ricci extends far beyond a single feast on February 13. Served as prioress of San Vincenzo convent in Prato for thirty-six years Canonized in 1746; known for wise spiritual counsel to bishops and princes Popes, councils, and local churches have confirmed this saint's importance through canonization, liturgical ranking, and the inclusion of their name in missals worldwide. Artists, composers, and writers continue to depict St. Catherine de' Ricci because holiness is attractive — it answers the deepest hunger of the human heart. For catechists and parents, this saint offers ready-made material: a story of virtue, a date to circle on the calendar, and a name to invoke at bedtime. For scholars, the same life opens windows into the history of Florence, Italy and the development of Catholic spirituality. In every case, the legacy is the same: St. Catherine de' Ricci still leads souls to Christ.",
