    "patronages": "St. Wulfric of Haselbury is widely invoked as patron of miracles, Somerset, and parish priests. Patron saints function in Catholic spirituality as elder siblings in faith: they have triumphed, they understand human weakness, and they present our needs before Christ. Choosing St. Wulfric of Haselbury as a heavenly advocate is never superstition; it is the communion of saints made practical. Miracles find particular comfort under this patronage, but the Church's calendar teaches that every saint overflows with grace for anyone who asks. On February 20, consider entrusting a specific intention — health, employment, conversion of a loved one, peace in your nation — to St. Wulfric of Haselbury. Many Catholics also name children, parishes, or apostolates after their patron saint, creating a lifelong bond of prayer and imitation.",
    "legacy": "The legacy of St. Wulfric of Haselbury extends far beyond a single feast on February 20. Known for prophecy and healing miracles throughout Somerset His cell became a pilgrimage site after his death Popes, councils, and local churches have confirmed this saint's importance through canonization, liturgical ranking, and the inclusion of their name in missals worldwide. Artists, composers, and writers continue to depict St. Wulfric of Haselbury because holiness is attractive — it answers the deepest hunger of the human heart. For catechists and parents, this saint offers ready-made material: a story of virtue, a date to circle on the calendar, and a name to invoke at bedtime. For scholars, the same life opens windows into the history of Compton, England and the development of Catholic spirituality. In every case, the legacy is the same: St. Wulfric of Haselbury still leads souls to Christ.",
