    "patronages": "St. Teresa of the Andes is widely invoked as patron of Chile, youth, and Discalced Carmelites. Patron saints function in Catholic spirituality as elder siblings in faith: they have triumphed, they understand human weakness, and they present our needs before Christ. Choosing St. Teresa of the Andes as a heavenly advocate is never superstition; it is the communion of saints made practical. Chile find particular comfort under this patronage, but the Church's calendar teaches that every saint overflows with grace for anyone who asks. On April 12, consider entrusting a specific intention — health, employment, conversion of a loved one, peace in your nation — to St. Teresa of the Andes. Many Catholics also name children, parishes, or apostolates after their patron saint, creating a lifelong bond of prayer and imitation.",
    "legacy": "The legacy of St. Teresa of the Andes extends far beyond a single feast on April 12. Her spiritual diary reveals extraordinary maturity and joy Canonized in 1993 by Pope John Paul II Popes, councils, and local churches have confirmed this saint's importance through canonization, liturgical ranking, and the inclusion of their name in missals worldwide. Artists, composers, and writers continue to depict St. Teresa of the Andes because holiness is attractive — it answers the deepest hunger of the human heart. For catechists and parents, this saint offers ready-made material: a story of virtue, a date to circle on the calendar, and a name to invoke at bedtime. For scholars, the same life opens windows into the history of Santiago, Chile and the development of Catholic spirituality. In every case, the legacy is the same: St. Teresa of the Andes still leads souls to Christ.",
