    "miraclesAndDevotion": "Devotion to St. George has flourished because the faithful experience this saint as a powerful intercessor before God. Patron of England, soldiers, scouts, and cavalry, St. George is invoked in novenas, parish feast-day celebrations, and private prayer — especially by those who see their struggles reflected in this saint's life. Roman soldier martyred under Diocletian for refusing to renounce Christ Legend of slaying a dragon symbolizes victory over evil Patron saint of England and many other nations and regions Shrines, relics, and icons associated with this saint attract pilgrims seeking healing, guidance, and conversion. Popular piety sometimes preserves customs linked to this feast: special foods, processions, school celebrations, or charitable collections for the causes this saint championed. The Church distinguishes between public revelation and private devotion, yet countless Catholics testify that praying through St. George opened doors when human effort alone failed. That living tradition — not merely historical curiosity — is why \"Saint of the Day\" features remain among the most searched Catholic topics online.",
    "patronages": "St. George is widely invoked as patron of England, soldiers, scouts, and cavalry. Patron saints function in Catholic spirituality as elder siblings in faith: they have triumphed, they understand human weakness, and they present our needs before Christ. Choosing St. George as a heavenly advocate is never superstition; it is the communion of saints made practical. England find particular comfort under this patronage, but the Church's calendar teaches that every saint overflows with grace for anyone who asks. On April 23, consider entrusting a specific intention — health, employment, conversion of a loved one, peace in your nation — to St. George. Many Catholics also name children, parishes, or apostolates after their patron saint, creating a lifelong bond of prayer and imitation.",
    "legacy": "The legacy of St. George extends far beyond a single feast on April 23. Patron saint of England and many other nations and regions One of the Fourteen Holy Helpers of medieval devotion Popes, councils, and local churches have confirmed this saint's importance through canonization, liturgical ranking, and the inclusion of their name in missals worldwide. Artists, composers, and writers continue to depict St. George because holiness is attractive — it answers the deepest hunger of the human heart. For catechists and parents, this saint offers ready-made material: a story of virtue, a date to circle on the calendar, and a name to invoke at bedtime. For scholars, the same life opens windows into the history of Lydda, Palestine and the development of Catholic spirituality. In every case, the legacy is the same: St. George still leads souls to Christ.",
